"Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666)\nciteturn32015academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\nTitle: Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\nAuthors: Stephan Rabanser, Sayash Kapoor, Peter Kirgis, Kangheng Liu, Saiteja Utpala, Arvind Narayanan\nDate: Wed Feb 18 18:05:44 2026\n\nAI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a fundamental limitation of current evaluations: compressing agent behavior into a single success metric obscures critical operational flaws. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity. Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile by proposing twelve concrete metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 14 agentic models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability. By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards a Science of AI Agent Reliability | arXiv Science (https://arxiv.science/abs/2602.16666)\nciteturn32015search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: today; Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666\n\narXiv · 2602.16666\n\n# Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\n\nStephan Rabanser·Sayash Kapoor·Peter Kirgis·Kangheng Liu·Saiteja Utpala·Arvind Narayanan\n\nView original source ↗Read source document ↗Export metadata ↓\n\n## Abstract\n\nAI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a fundamental limitation of current evaluations: compressing agent behavior into a single success metric obscures critical operational flaws. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity. Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile by proposing twelve concrete metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 15 models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability. By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.\n\nRECORD DETAILS\n\nSource\n    arXiv\nPublication date\n    2026-06-02\nSource updated\n    2026-06-03\nReuse permissions\n    http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\n\nMetadata is provided by the original archive. This page does not imply that the source document or image is freely reusable.\n\nStephan Rabanser, Sayash Kapoor, Peter Kirgis, Kangheng Liu, Saiteja Utpala, Arvind Narayanan. 2026-06-02. Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666\n\nCite the original work for its findings. Save a collection to share your selection of sources.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards a Science of AI Agent Reliability (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/rabanser26a.html)\nciteturn32015search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; `@InProceedings{pmlr-v306-rabanser26a, title = {Towards a Science of {AI} Agent Reliability}, author = {Rabanser, Stephan and Kapoor, Sayash and Kirgis, Peter and Liu, Kangheng and Utpala, Saiteja and Narayanan, Arvind}, booktitle = {Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning}, pages = {102769--102820}, year = {2026}, editor = {Zhang, Tong and Dudik, Miroslav and Jaggi, Martin and Agarwal, Alekh and Li, Sharon and Schuurmans, Dale and Zhu, Jerry and Berkenkamp, Felix and Dong, Hanze and Bietti, Alberto}, volume = {306}, series = {Proceedings of Machine Learning Research}, month = {06--11 Jul}, publisher = {PMLR}, pdf = {https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v306/main/assets/rabanser26a/rabanser26a.pdf}, url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/rabanser26a.html}, abstract = {AI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks.\n\n# Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\n\nStephan Rabanser, Sayash Kapoor, Peter Kirgis, Kangheng Liu, Saiteja Utpala, Arvind Narayanan\n\nProceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 306:102769-102820, 2026.\n\n#### Abstract\n\nAI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a major limitation of current evaluations: focusing on a single metric is not enough to understand agent behavior. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity. Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile consisting of twelve metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 15 models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability. By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.\n\n#### Cite this Paper\n\n* * *\n\nBibTeX\n\n`@InProceedings{pmlr-v306-rabanser26a, title = {Towards a Science of {AI} Agent Reliability}, author = {Rabanser, Stephan and Kapoor, Sayash and Kirgis, Peter and Liu, Kangheng and Utpala, Saiteja and Narayanan, Arvind}, booktitle = {Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning}, pages = {102769--102820}, year = {2026}, editor = {Zhang, Tong and Dudik, Miroslav and Jaggi, Martin and Agarwal, Alekh and Li, Sharon and Schuurmans, Dale and Zhu, Jerry and Berkenkamp, Felix and Dong, Hanze and Bietti, Alberto}, volume = {306}, series = {Proceedings of Machine Learning Research}, month = {06--11 Jul}, publisher = {PMLR}, pdf = {https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v306/main/assets/rabanser26a/rabanser26a.pdf}, url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/rabanser26a.html}, abstract = {AI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a major limitation of current evaluations: focusing on a single metric is not enough to understand agent behavior. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity. Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile consisting of twelve metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 15 models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability. By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.} } `\n\nEndnote\n\n`%0 Conference